        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "If there\u2019s pain in the front of the finger when crimping, but no sign of bowstringing, what does that suggest? Could it be less serious than an A2 pulley injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Pain in the front of the finger when crimping, without signs of bowstringing, may suggest a less severe injury than an A2 pulley injury. It could indicate a strain or irritation of the tendons or ligaments in the finger, which is common in climbers, especially if the pain is localized and not accompanied by significant swelling or deformity. It's important to monitor the pain; if it persists or worsens, consider consulting a healthcare professional for a thorough evaluation and appropriate treatment.",
