    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s useful. I get this pain mostly at the front of the finger when I\u2019m crimping, but I can\u2019t see any bowstring or big swelling. What kind of injury could that be?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Based on your description, the pain at the front of your finger during crimping without visible bowstringing or significant swelling could indicate a mild strain or partial injury to the flexor tendon pulleys, particularly the A2 pulley, which is common among climbers. These injuries often occur due to the high forces exerted during crimping, especially if the grip is fully closed. While bowstringing is a sign of more severe pulley damage, its absence suggests the injury might be less severe. To manage this, you should rest the finger, avoid crimping for a while, and consider light massage to improve blood flow. Gradual reintroduction of climbing with proper warm-up and technique can help prevent further issues. If the pain persists or worsens, consulting a medical professional is recommended to rule out more serious conditions.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 40.353599548339844
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors."
