  {
    "runtag": "genaius-summary-rewrite",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m looking for info on how to tell different finger injuries apart. Any tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Finger injuries in climbing can be tricky to differentiate, but there are some common signs to look out for. Pulley injuries, for example, often involve a \"pop\" sound followed by pain at the base of the finger. Tendon strains might feel like a dull ache or sharp pain when gripping. If you experience swelling or bruising, it could indicate a more severe injury. For any injury, it\u2019s crucial to rest and avoid climbing until you have a clear diagnosis. Consulting a hand specialist or physical therapist experienced with climbing injuries is highly recommended. They can provide tailored advice for recovery and rehabilitation. Let me know if you\u2019d like tips on prevention or strengthening exercises to avoid future injuries.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:0": 18.294700622558594
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.",
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, thanks. What if there\u2019s pain at the front of the finger, mainly when crimping, but no visible bowstring? Any idea what that points to?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Pain at the front of the finger during crimping, without visible bowstringing, could indicate a strain or partial injury to the A2 pulley, which is common among climbers. The A2 pulley is under significant stress during crimping, especially in a full crimp grip. While bowstringing is a sign of more severe injuries, the absence of it doesn\u2019t rule out a less severe strain. This type of pain can also result from overuse or improper warm-up before climbing. \n\nTo address this, consider resting the finger and avoiding crimp-intensive routes for a while. Gradually reintroduce climbing with a focus on open-hand or half-crimp grips, which are less stressful on the pulleys. If the pain persists or worsens, consulting a specialist is recommended to rule out more serious issues. Proper warm-up and technique, as well as finger-strengthening exercises, can help prevent future injuries.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 34.995201110839844,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 27.018400192260742
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Could icing or taping help, or should I just rest it? Also, is there a risk for long-term damage if I climb too soon?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Icing can help reduce inflammation and manage pain in the early stages of a finger injury. Apply ice for 15-20 minutes at a time, ensuring you take breaks between sessions to avoid skin damage. Taping, specifically H-taping, can provide support to the injured pulley by keeping the tendons close to the bone, which may help during light, non-painful climbing or daily activities. However, taping should not be a substitute for rest and proper recovery.\n\nRest is crucial for healing, especially for pulley injuries. Climbing too soon can increase the risk of long-term damage, such as chronic pain or further tearing, which could lead to extended downtime or even surgical intervention. To minimize risks, avoid crimping and high-intensity climbing until the pain subsides and the finger regains strength. If the pain persists or worsens, consult a climbing-knowledgeable physician or physical therapist for a tailored recovery plan.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:1": 30.57830047607422,
            "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 28.25200080871582,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 25.754600524902344,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 23.497499465942383
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s clear. What kinds of long-term issues might happen if I don\u2019t give my finger enough time to heal before climbing again?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the risk if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "If you resume climbing before your finger has fully healed, you risk developing chronic issues that could significantly impact your climbing. Long-term problems may include persistent pain, reduced grip strength, and limited range of motion in the injured finger. Additionally, the injury could worsen, leading to a complete rupture of the pulley, which might require surgical intervention and a much longer recovery period. Over time, insufficient healing can also lead to compensatory injuries in other fingers or joints as your body tries to adapt to the weakened area. To avoid these outcomes, it\u2019s crucial to allow adequate time for recovery and follow a structured rehabilitation plan.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 32.338401794433594,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 32.2963981628418,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 30.760799407958984
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s useful. What about when I do start climbing again\u2014any advice specifically for returning after a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "When returning to climbing after a finger injury, it\u2019s crucial to ease back in gradually to avoid re-injury. Start with easier routes that don\u2019t require intense crimping or small holds. Focus on using open-hand or half-crimp grips, as these are less stressful on the pulleys. Warm up thoroughly with aerobic activity, dynamic stretching, and light climbing to prepare your fingers and tendons for the load. \n\nConsider taping the injured finger for added support, but don\u2019t rely on it to push harder than your finger is ready for. Pay close attention to any pain or discomfort\u2014this is your body\u2019s way of signaling that you might be overdoing it. \n\nAdditionally, incorporating finger-strengthening exercises, like controlled hangs on larger holds using open-hand or half-crimp positions, can help rebuild strength safely. Avoid full crimping until you\u2019re fully recovered. If you\u2019re unsure about your progress, consulting a climbing-specific physical therapist can provide tailored guidance. \n\nRemember, patience is key to ensuring a full recovery and preventing long-term issues.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 25.059600830078125,
            "clueweb22-en0044-90-18231:2": 23.88010025024414,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 22.667299270629883
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Good tips, thank you. If I want to tape my finger, especially at the front where the pain is, what\u2019s the best method?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To tape your finger for pain at the front, particularly if it\u2019s related to an A2 pulley injury, the H-taping method is highly recommended. This method provides substantial support to the pulley by keeping the tendons close to the bone. Here\u2019s how to do it:\n\n1. Cut a piece of rigid athletic tape about 10 cm long and 1.5 cm wide.\n2. Cut the tape lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1 cm bridge in the middle.\n3. Place the tape on the injured finger with the bridge positioned over the crease of the joint near the injured pulley.\n4. Flex your finger slightly (around 60\u00b0) and wrap the upper part of the tape around the finger to form a ring above the joint.\n5. Wrap the lower part of the tape below the joint to form another ring.\n\nThis method helps reduce the load on the injured pulley while climbing and supports recovery. Remember to retape between climbs as the tape can stretch and lose effectiveness. If pain persists, consult a specialist before resuming intense climbing activities.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 28.273399353027344,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 27.34040069580078,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 23.200199127197266
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
