          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, thanks. What if there\u2019s pain at the front of the finger, mainly when crimping, but no visible bowstring? Any idea what that points to?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Pain at the front of the finger during crimping, without visible bowstringing, could indicate a strain or partial injury to the A2 pulley, which is common among climbers. The A2 pulley is under significant stress during crimping, especially in a full crimp grip. While bowstringing is a sign of more severe injuries, the absence of it doesn\u2019t rule out a less severe strain. This type of pain can also result from overuse or improper warm-up before climbing. \n\nTo address this, consider resting the finger and avoiding crimp-intensive routes for a while. Gradually reintroduce climbing with a focus on open-hand or half-crimp grips, which are less stressful on the pulleys. If the pain persists or worsens, consulting a specialist is recommended to rule out more serious issues. Proper warm-up and technique, as well as finger-strengthening exercises, can help prevent future injuries.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 34.995201110839844,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 27.018400192260742
          },
