  {
    "runtag": "genaius-summary-rewrite",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Could icing or taping help, or should I just rest it? Also, is there a risk for long-term damage if I climb too soon?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Icing can help reduce inflammation and manage pain in the early stages of a finger injury. Apply ice for 15-20 minutes at a time, ensuring you take breaks between sessions to avoid skin damage. Taping, specifically H-taping, can provide support to the injured pulley by keeping the tendons close to the bone, which may help during light, non-painful climbing or daily activities. However, taping should not be a substitute for rest and proper recovery.\n\nRest is crucial for healing, especially for pulley injuries. Climbing too soon can increase the risk of long-term damage, such as chronic pain or further tearing, which could lead to extended downtime or even surgical intervention. To minimize risks, avoid crimping and high-intensity climbing until the pain subsides and the finger regains strength. If the pain persists or worsens, consult a climbing-knowledgeable physician or physical therapist for a tailored recovery plan.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:1": 30.57830047607422,
            "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 28.25200080871582,
