  {
    "runtag": "genaius-summary-rewrite",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s useful. What about when I do start climbing again\u2014any advice specifically for returning after a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "When returning to climbing after a finger injury, it\u2019s crucial to ease back in gradually to avoid re-injury. Start with easier routes that don\u2019t require intense crimping or small holds. Focus on using open-hand or half-crimp grips, as these are less stressful on the pulleys. Warm up thoroughly with aerobic activity, dynamic stretching, and light climbing to prepare your fingers and tendons for the load. \n\nConsider taping the injured finger for added support, but don\u2019t rely on it to push harder than your finger is ready for. Pay close attention to any pain or discomfort\u2014this is your body\u2019s way of signaling that you might be overdoing it. \n\nAdditionally, incorporating finger-strengthening exercises, like controlled hangs on larger holds using open-hand or half-crimp positions, can help rebuild strength safely. Avoid full crimping until you\u2019re fully recovered. If you\u2019re unsure about your progress, consulting a climbing-specific physical therapist can provide tailored guidance. \n\nRemember, patience is key to ensuring a full recovery and preventing long-term issues.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 25.059600830078125,
            "clueweb22-en0044-90-18231:2": 23.88010025024414,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 22.667299270629883
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
