  {
    "runtag": "genaius-summary-rewrite",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Good tips, thank you. If I want to tape my finger, especially at the front where the pain is, what\u2019s the best method?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To tape your finger for pain at the front, particularly if it\u2019s related to an A2 pulley injury, the H-taping method is highly recommended. This method provides substantial support to the pulley by keeping the tendons close to the bone. Here\u2019s how to do it:\n\n1. Cut a piece of rigid athletic tape about 10 cm long and 1.5 cm wide.\n2. Cut the tape lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1 cm bridge in the middle.\n3. Place the tape on the injured finger with the bridge positioned over the crease of the joint near the injured pulley.\n4. Flex your finger slightly (around 60\u00b0) and wrap the upper part of the tape around the finger to form a ring above the joint.\n5. Wrap the lower part of the tape below the joint to form another ring.\n\nThis method helps reduce the load on the injured pulley while climbing and supports recovery. Remember to retape between climbs as the tape can stretch and lose effectiveness. If pain persists, consult a specialist before resuming intense climbing activities.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 28.273399353027344,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 27.34040069580078,
