  {
    "runtag": "grilllab-agentic-gpt4.1-larf-v2",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m looking for ways to tell different finger injuries apart. What signs should I look for?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To tell different finger injuries apart, look for these signs: A sprain typically results in swelling, pain, tenderness, and reduced flexibility, but the finger usually maintains its normal shape unless the injury is severe [DOC-1][DOC-3]. A fracture (broken finger) often causes more intense pain, swelling, bruising, and sometimes visible deformity or instability of the finger, especially if the break is severe [DOC-1]. Dislocations are characterized by a crooked or deformed finger, inability to move the joint, significant pain, swelling, and sometimes numbness or tingling if nerves are affected [DOC-2][DOC-4]. Jammed fingers can involve sprains, dislocations, or fractures, so symptoms may overlap, but persistent deformity or inability to move the finger suggests a more serious injury like a dislocation or fracture [DOC-3][DOC-4]. X-rays are often needed for a definitive diagnosis, especially if there is deformity or loss of function. When should I see a doctor for a finger injury?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-96-17361:1": 0.05914374323098921,
            "clueweb22-en0041-56-06837:8": 0.05700204813108039,
            "clueweb22-en0011-49-07381:0": 0.04836297977911243,
            "clueweb22-en0041-56-06837:6": 0.04466340526821057
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful. If the pain is mainly when crimping, at the front of the finger, with no bowstringing\u2014what injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Pain at the front of the finger when crimping, without bowstringing, is most commonly associated with a partial A2 pulley injury, which is frequent in climbers. How can I safely return to climbing after a finger pulley injury?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.06557377049180328,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 0.06452452301209573,
