            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great, that sounds familiar. Is taping the finger recommended for getting back into climbing, or should I just rest it?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Taping is commonly used by climbers after a finger pulley injury, such as an A2 injury, to provide support and potentially help keep the tendons close to the bone, especially if there is no bowstringing. Research by Sch\u00f6ffl et al. compared different taping methods and found that while taping can offer some mechanical support, its main benefit may be as a reminder to avoid overusing the finger and to restrict movement, rather than as a substitute for proper rest [DOC-1][DOC-7]. Some experts argue that taping should intentionally limit finger bending to prevent further injury, but the consensus is that rest and a gradual return to climbing are crucial for healing. Taping should not be relied upon to allow a full return to normal climbing immediately after injury; instead, it can be used as an adjunct to a carefully managed rehabilitation process [DOC-1]. Are there specific exercises to help rehabilitate a finger pulley injury?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": 0.05957049486461251,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:2": 0.04841188524590165,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 0.045799361928394185,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:1": 0.045235361653272105
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s just what I was wondering about. Which exercises are best for building back finger strength safely after an A2 pulley injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "After an A2 pulley injury, start with gentle range-of-motion exercises\u2014bending and straightening the finger until it feels tight, holding for a few seconds, and repeating several times daily. As pain subsides, you can add resistance exercises such as squeezing therapy putty, using rubber bands for finger extension, or manual resistance with your other hand. Deloaded hangboard work (using pulleys or assistance to reduce load) is also recommended to gradually reintroduce finger strength, but only if it is pain-free. Rice bucket exercises and light finger curls with a small dumbbell can help as well. The key is to progress slowly, avoid pain, and not rush back to full climbing intensity to prevent reinjury. Always consult a medical professional for a tailored rehab plan, especially if pain persists or worsens [clueweb22-en0007-15-09796][clueweb22-en0015-71-10781]. How can I tell if I'm progressing too quickly in my rehab?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:15": 0.058879332866102904,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 0.04918032786885246,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:13": 0.0452122777796662,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": 0.0315136476426799
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "So, what are the risks if I keep climbing and don\u2019t rest enough with this kind of injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the risk if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Continuing to climb without adequate rest after an A2 pulley injury can lead to several serious risks. Tendons and pulleys heal much more slowly than muscles, and insufficient rest can turn a minor strain or partial tear into a full rupture, which may result in visible bowstringing and require a much longer recovery\u2014often at least 6 weeks or more off climbing [clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:7]. Chronic overuse without rest can also cause tendinosis, a degenerative condition that is much harder to treat and can sideline you for months [clueweb22-en0006-07-05326:1]. In some cases, persistent climbing on an injured finger can lead to long-term weakness, pain, or even permanent loss of function. Proper rest and a gradual return to activity are essential to avoid these complications. How can I prevent finger injuries in the future?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:7": 0.032266458495966696,
            "clueweb22-en0039-47-17937:5": 0.031099324975891997,
            "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:6": 0.031054405392392875,
            "clueweb22-en0006-07-05326:1": 0.030536130536130537
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s a good reminder. Are warm-up routines actually effective at reducing finger injuries before climbing, or are there better ways to prevent them?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Scientific studies and expert recommendations support the effectiveness of warm-up routines in preventing finger injuries among climbers. A proper warm-up should start with aerobic activity (like jogging or a brisk approach), followed by dynamic stretching, and then easy, sport-specific climbing. Research has shown that warming up increases the pliability of the finger flexor tendons, allowing them to absorb more force and reducing the risk of injury [clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:4][clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:5]. Additionally, focusing on climbing technique\u2014such as avoiding dynamic moves to crimps and using precise footwork\u2014further decreases injury risk. Activating both the flexor and extensor muscles in the forearm before climbing also helps maintain stability and prevent overuse injuries [clueweb22-en0044-90-13131:5]. Static stretching is best saved for after climbing or rest days, as it may reduce performance if done beforehand. What are some specific dynamic warm-up exercises for climbers?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:4": 0.0635164584959667,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:5": 0.062160778289810543,
            "clueweb22-en0044-90-13131:5": 0.06154574592074592,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.060237137508312374
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s useful. Can you tell me about taping methods? What\u2019s the best way to tape the front of my finger after this kind of injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "The H-taping method is widely recommended for A2 pulley injuries, as it provides targeted support to the front of the finger and is more effective than simple circumferential taping. Are there specific brands of tape recommended for finger injuries?",
