  {
    "runtag": "grilllab-agentic-gpt4.1-larf-v2",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s a good reminder. Are warm-up routines actually effective at reducing finger injuries before climbing, or are there better ways to prevent them?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Scientific studies and expert recommendations support the effectiveness of warm-up routines in preventing finger injuries among climbers. A proper warm-up should start with aerobic activity (like jogging or a brisk approach), followed by dynamic stretching, and then easy, sport-specific climbing. Research has shown that warming up increases the pliability of the finger flexor tendons, allowing them to absorb more force and reducing the risk of injury [clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:4][clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:5]. Additionally, focusing on climbing technique\u2014such as avoiding dynamic moves to crimps and using precise footwork\u2014further decreases injury risk. Activating both the flexor and extensor muscles in the forearm before climbing also helps maintain stability and prevent overuse injuries [clueweb22-en0044-90-13131:5]. Static stretching is best saved for after climbing or rest days, as it may reduce performance if done beforehand. What are some specific dynamic warm-up exercises for climbers?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:4": 0.0635164584959667,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:5": 0.062160778289810543,
