  {
    "runtag": "grilllab-agentic-gpt4.1-larf",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m looking for ways to tell different kinds of finger injuries apart. Can you give me some tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "A sprained finger typically involves injury to the ligaments and presents with pain, redness, swelling, bruising, and reduced mobility. You may notice throbbing, especially at rest, and difficulty straightening or bending the finger, but the finger usually remains aligned [DOC-3][DOC-2]. In contrast, a broken (fractured) finger involves injury to the bone and often causes more severe pain, significant swelling, and sometimes visible deformity or abnormal bending. Broken fingers are usually very difficult or impossible to move without extreme pain, and the finger may appear out of alignment [DOC-3][DOC-2][DOC-4]. Dislocations occur when the joint is forced out of its normal position, often resulting in obvious deformity, severe instability, and sometimes stretching of the ligaments. Dislocations may also be accompanied by secondary fractures and require prompt medical attention to restore alignment [DOC-1]. If you experience severe pain, deformity, or inability to move the finger, seek medical care immediately. When should I see a doctor for a finger injury?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-96-17361:7": 0.05442589314459349,
            "clueweb22-en0039-62-17312:3": 0.04810956200662083,
            "clueweb22-en0039-62-17312:2": 0.047357028935976306,
            "clueweb22-en0034-96-17361:1": 0.04447671049412128
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s useful, thanks! If the pain is just at the front of my finger while crimping, but there\u2019s no bowstring, what kind of injury could that be?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Front-of-finger pain during crimping, especially without visible bowstringing, is usually due to a strain or partial injury of the finger's annular pulleys (most often the A2 pulley) rather than a complete rupture. Pulley strains are much more likely with a full crimp grip and present as pain on the front of the finger, but bowstringing (a bulge at the base of the finger) only occurs with more severe, complete ruptures [DOC-1][DOC-2][DOC-3][DOC-4]. Flexor tendon injuries can also cause pain along the palm side of the finger, but these are less common and often involve an inability to bend the finger or numbness, which you did not mention [DOC-2]. If the pain is mild and there is no deformity or loss of function, it is likely a minor pulley strain, but persistent or worsening symptoms should be evaluated by a medical professional. How can I safely return to climbing after a pulley strain?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 0.08091796563504655,
            "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 0.07461427461427461,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.06453291699193339,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 0.061118093376157895
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, cheers. What are some good ways to keep climbing while letting a finger pulley strain heal?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "The most effective way to protect a healing pulley while climbing is to use H-taping, which supports the injured pulley and helps keep the tendons close to the bone, reducing strain during flexion and crimping. Research shows H-taping can increase crimping strength in injured fingers and is more effective than other taping methods [DOC-2][DOC-10]. It's important to retape between climbs, as tape can loosen quickly. Some experts recommend taping specifically to restrict your ability to crimp, essentially forcing you to use open-hand grips and avoid positions that stress the pulley [DOC-12][DOC-13]. You should also climb on easier routes, avoid moves that require hard crimping, and listen to your body\u2014if pain increases, rest is essential. These strategies allow you to stay active while minimizing the risk of worsening the injury. Do you want advice on finger strengthening or rehab exercises?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 0.046634615384615385,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.04447671049412128,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 0.043561840413764236,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 0.04220307651680201
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that's clear. What are the risks if I keep climbing and don\u2019t rest with a front-of-finger injury like this?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the risk if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "If you keep climbing on a finger with a pulley strain, you risk escalating the injury from a minor strain to a full tear, especially if you continue to use crimp grips that put high force on the pulleys. Chronic overuse without adequate rest can lead to persistent pain, tendinosis, or even acute ruptures that may require months of recovery or surgical intervention [DOC-1][DOC-7]. Tendons and pulleys heal much more slowly than muscles due to limited blood flow, so insufficient rest can prolong healing or cause permanent weakness and reduced finger function [DOC-7]. In severe cases, you may develop bowstringing or lose the ability to fully bend your finger, which can significantly impact your climbing and daily activities [DOC-6]. How long should I rest before returning to climbing?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0006-07-05326:1": 0.041690738542662364,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.032266458495966696,
            "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:7": 0.031544957774465976,
            "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 0.031009615384615385
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Good to know\u2014I never want to be off climbing for months! How do climbers mentally cope with taking time off for finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Taking time off for a finger injury can be mentally challenging for climbers, but several psychological strategies can help. Setting small, achievable rehab goals and visualizing your recovery progress can boost confidence and reduce anxiety about returning to climbing [DOC-4][DOC-3]. Techniques such as deep breathing, relaxation, and guided imagery are shown to help regulate emotions and reduce the emotional impact of injury [DOC-2][DOC-3]. Staying connected with your climbing community and also seeking support outside of it can provide encouragement and perspective during recovery [DOC-4]. Many athletes also use the downtime to work on weaknesses, such as mental training, flexibility, or core strength, so they return both physically and mentally stronger [DOC-4]. Do you want tips on how to set effective rehab goals?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0023-77-07377:0": 0.029850746268656716,
            "clueweb22-en0013-17-02410:4": 0.0251661918328585,
            "clueweb22-en0041-65-03719:2": 0.024922600619195046,
            "clueweb22-en0006-53-16559:4": 0.02424331472230385
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Actually, I\u2019m more interested in how to avoid re-injury once I\u2019m back. What habits help with long-term finger health for people who climb outside?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For long-term finger health and to prevent re-injury, outdoor climbers should always start with a thorough warm-up, including aerobic activity (like hiking to the crag), dynamic stretching, and easy climbing before attempting harder moves. Proper technique\u2014especially avoiding overuse of the full crimp grip and focusing on good footwork and body positioning\u2014significantly reduces finger strain. Gradually increasing the intensity and volume of your climbing, rather than making sudden jumps, helps your tendons and pulleys adapt safely [DOC-1]. Incorporating a daily or regular 'protective training' protocol, such as moderate-intensity finger exercises designed to strengthen tendons and pulleys, can further reduce injury risk and promote tendon health [DOC-4]. Full-body training, including core, leg, and upper-body strength, as well as regular warm-downs, also supports finger health by improving overall climbing efficiency and reducing the load on your fingers [DOC-2][DOC-5]. Would you like specific warm-up or protective finger training routines?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.04841188524590164,
            "clueweb22-en0012-12-14907:6": 0.048388472530888,
            "clueweb22-en0012-13-03482:2": 0.03869565217391305,
            "clueweb22-en0044-90-18231:0": 0.03846575365562707,
            "clueweb22-en0012-12-14907:7": 0.035564346120349345
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Warm-up routines would be great. Also, is there anything about taping technique I should watch out for, especially if pain is at the front of my finger near the base?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To prevent finger injuries outdoors, start your warm-up with 5\u201310 minutes of aerobic exercise (like hiking to the crag or jumping jacks) to increase blood flow, followed by dynamic stretching for wrists and fingers\u2014such as alternating wrist flexion/extension and gentle finger movements for about 6 minutes [DOC-4]. Before hard climbing, use easy routes or a fingerboard to gradually load your fingers, focusing on open-hand grips [DOC-5][DOC-14]. For taping, especially for pain at the front near the base (A2 pulley), H-taping is most effective: it supports the pulley and keeps tendons close to the bone. Alternatively, tape from halfway between the first and second finger creases to halfway between the second and third, overlapping 50%, to restrict crimping and protect the pulley [DOC-12]. Avoid taping too tightly, which can restrict blood flow or cause the finger to bend unnaturally. Taping is for support during recovery, not for prevention in healthy fingers [DOC-5].",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0039-62-17312:9": 0.04079390875165523,
