    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Good to know\u2014I never want to be off climbing for months! How do climbers mentally cope with taking time off for finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Taking time off for a finger injury can be mentally challenging for climbers, but several psychological strategies can help. Setting small, achievable rehab goals and visualizing your recovery progress can boost confidence and reduce anxiety about returning to climbing [DOC-4][DOC-3]. Techniques such as deep breathing, relaxation, and guided imagery are shown to help regulate emotions and reduce the emotional impact of injury [DOC-2][DOC-3]. Staying connected with your climbing community and also seeking support outside of it can provide encouragement and perspective during recovery [DOC-4]. Many athletes also use the downtime to work on weaknesses, such as mental training, flexibility, or core strength, so they return both physically and mentally stronger [DOC-4]. Do you want tips on how to set effective rehab goals?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0023-77-07377:0": 0.029850746268656716,
            "clueweb22-en0013-17-02410:4": 0.0251661918328585,
