          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Actually, I\u2019m more interested in how to avoid re-injury once I\u2019m back. What habits help with long-term finger health for people who climb outside?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For long-term finger health and to prevent re-injury, outdoor climbers should always start with a thorough warm-up, including aerobic activity (like hiking to the crag), dynamic stretching, and easy climbing before attempting harder moves. Proper technique\u2014especially avoiding overuse of the full crimp grip and focusing on good footwork and body positioning\u2014significantly reduces finger strain. Gradually increasing the intensity and volume of your climbing, rather than making sudden jumps, helps your tendons and pulleys adapt safely [DOC-1]. Incorporating a daily or regular 'protective training' protocol, such as moderate-intensity finger exercises designed to strengthen tendons and pulleys, can further reduce injury risk and promote tendon health [DOC-4]. Full-body training, including core, leg, and upper-body strength, as well as regular warm-downs, also supports finger health by improving overall climbing efficiency and reducing the load on your fingers [DOC-2][DOC-5]. Would you like specific warm-up or protective finger training routines?",
          "citations": {
