  {
    "runtag": "grilllab-agentic-gpt4.1-larf",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Warm-up routines would be great. Also, is there anything about taping technique I should watch out for, especially if pain is at the front of my finger near the base?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To prevent finger injuries outdoors, start your warm-up with 5\u201310 minutes of aerobic exercise (like hiking to the crag or jumping jacks) to increase blood flow, followed by dynamic stretching for wrists and fingers\u2014such as alternating wrist flexion/extension and gentle finger movements for about 6 minutes [DOC-4]. Before hard climbing, use easy routes or a fingerboard to gradually load your fingers, focusing on open-hand grips [DOC-5][DOC-14]. For taping, especially for pain at the front near the base (A2 pulley), H-taping is most effective: it supports the pulley and keeps tendons close to the bone. Alternatively, tape from halfway between the first and second finger creases to halfway between the second and third, overlapping 50%, to restrict crimping and protect the pulley [DOC-12]. Avoid taping too tightly, which can restrict blood flow or cause the finger to bend unnaturally. Taping is for support during recovery, not for prevention in healthy fingers [DOC-5].",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0039-62-17312:9": 0.04079390875165523,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 0.03252247488101534,
