  {
    "runtag": "grilllab-agentic-gpt4.1",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019d like to know how to tell the difference between finger injuries. What should I look for?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Finger sprains usually result from hyperextension or impact, causing pain, swelling, redness, bruising, and throbbing, but the finger remains stable and symptoms improve with rest over 1-2 weeks. A fracture (broken finger) involves direct injury to the bone, often with more severe pain, possible deformity, instability, and sometimes a visible misalignment or inability to move the finger properly; fractures may also cause significant swelling and bruising and require medical attention [DOC-1][DOC-2]. Tendon injuries, common in climbers, can present as a sudden 'pop' with pain at the base of the finger, swelling, and sometimes bowstringing (bulging) when bending the finger. Flexor tendon injuries may cause an inability to bend the finger, pain when trying to move it, and tenderness along the palm side; these injuries often need prompt medical evaluation [DOC-2][DOC-3]. If you notice deformity, loss of movement, numbness, or bowstringing, seek medical advice promptly.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-96-17361:1": 0.046938305082179284,
            "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 0.04460380182903154,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.042773940672513236,
            "clueweb22-en0034-96-17361:2": 0.04195816675129223
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful. What if the pain is near the front of the finger, specifically when crimping? There\u2019s no visible bowstring, just pain. Any idea what that could mean?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Crimping places significant stress on the finger's flexor tendon pulleys, especially the A2 pulley, which is the most commonly injured in climbers. Pain at the base or front of the finger during crimping, even without visible bowstringing, often suggests a partial A2 pulley strain or minor tear. Bowstringing typically appears only in more severe (grade 4) ruptures, so its absence does not rule out a pulley injury. Other symptoms can include tenderness, swelling, and pain when bending or straightening the finger, especially under load. If you experience a sudden 'pop,' significant swelling, or loss of function, it's important to seek medical advice, but milder cases often present as pain during crimping without obvious deformity [DOC-1][DOC-3]. How can I prevent finger injuries when climbing?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.07969243265548132,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 0.07664267051923755,
            "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 0.05259266949305441,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.05175626713670192
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019ve actually had finger pain like this before, and it really affected my climbing. Are taping techniques effective at preventing these kinds of pulley injuries?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Research shows that taping healthy fingers does not meaningfully reduce the risk of pulley injuries and may even be counterproductive, as it can prevent your pulleys from adapting and strengthening properly (Schweizer, 2000). Taping is most beneficial during recovery from an existing pulley injury, where it can help support the injured area and reduce load, especially when using the H-taping method, which has been shown to be more effective than other techniques in keeping tendons close to the bone and supporting injured pulleys during crimping. H-taping can also increase crimping strength in injured fingers by about 13% and may help prevent further damage while you heal. For prevention, focus on proper warm-up, progressive loading, and good technique rather than taping healthy fingers [DOC-1][DOC-6][DOC-7][DOC-8]. Are you interested in other injury prevention strategies for climbers?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 0.06302805800756621,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 0.06273601418133337,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.06254039602796874,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 0.062296404880360104
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yes, I\u2019d like to know about specific warm-up routines or exercises you\u2019d recommend for climbers to avoid finger problems. Anything simple for someone who mainly climbs outdoors?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "To prevent finger injuries when climbing outdoors, start with 10\u201320 minutes of aerobic activity\u2014your approach hike is ideal\u2014to increase blood flow and warm up your muscles and tendons [DOC-1][DOC-3]. Next, perform dynamic stretches for your arms, wrists, and fingers, such as arm circles, wrist rotations, and gentle finger flicks or spreads. Once at the crag, begin with easy climbing: do 8\u201312 easy boulder problems or 3\u20134 easy routes, focusing on smooth movement and technique rather than intensity [DOC-1][DOC-4]. If you have a portable fingerboard or resistance bands, use them for gentle, progressive loading of your fingers before tackling harder problems. Avoid jumping straight onto crimpy or finger-intensive routes, and try to use open-hand or half-crimp grips instead of full crimping when possible to reduce stress on your pulleys [DOC-4]. Are you interested in fingerboard warm-up protocols for outdoor use?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.07711343947197993,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:4": 0.07564839773077539,
            "clueweb22-en0044-90-13131:0": 0.07350689932091241,
            "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:14": 0.06884684573023464
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Actually, I don\u2019t have a portable fingerboard, and I usually just warm up with easy climbing. Is that enough, or should I get something like a grip trainer or a band? Also, are there signs that I might be pushing my fingers too far during warm-ups?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Warming up with easy climbing\u2014such as 8\u201312 easy boulder problems or 3\u20134 easy routes\u2014is effective for preparing your fingers and reducing injury risk, especially when combined with an aerobic approach hike and dynamic stretching [DOC-3]. However, using resistance bands or grip trainers before finger-intensive climbs can provide additional targeted warm-up and is recommended by many experts, particularly if you plan to attempt crimpy or hard routes [DOC-1][DOC-2][DOC-4]. Signs that you may be pushing your fingers too far during warm-up include sharp or acute pain, a 'tweak' sensation, persistent discomfort, or swelling. If you experience any of these, it's best to stop, avoid hard crimping, and give your fingers time to recover [DOC-1][DOC-2]. Would you like recommendations for specific grip trainers or resistance bands?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 0.04477114477114477,
            "clueweb22-en0014-52-01833:3": 0.044223148327625944,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.04334117590856433,
            "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:14": 0.042459043688551884
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I climb outdoors.",
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s useful, thanks. I have a bit of swelling right at the joint after my last session, but no \u201cpop.\u201d Should I keep climbing, or take a break for a while?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the risk if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Swelling at the finger joint after climbing, even without a 'pop,' suggests a mild strain or overuse. Experts recommend resting from climbing until pain and swelling resolve, which may take 1\u20134 weeks depending on severity. During this time, gentle massage, alternating heat and cold, and light finger exercises (like squeezing a stress ball) can aid recovery. Once symptoms improve, you can return to easy climbing on large holds, keeping sessions short and avoiding crimpy or overhanging routes. If easy climbing causes no pain, you can gradually increase intensity. Persistent swelling or pain may indicate a more serious issue, so consult a specialist if symptoms don't improve [clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:5][clueweb22-en0042-26-18398:3]. Would you like tips on specific recovery exercises for finger injuries?",
          "citations": {
