      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful. What if the pain is near the front of the finger, specifically when crimping? There\u2019s no visible bowstring, just pain. Any idea what that could mean?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Crimping places significant stress on the finger's flexor tendon pulleys, especially the A2 pulley, which is the most commonly injured in climbers. Pain at the base or front of the finger during crimping, even without visible bowstringing, often suggests a partial A2 pulley strain or minor tear. Bowstringing typically appears only in more severe (grade 4) ruptures, so its absence does not rule out a pulley injury. Other symptoms can include tenderness, swelling, and pain when bending or straightening the finger, especially under load. If you experience a sudden 'pop,' significant swelling, or loss of function, it's important to seek medical advice, but milder cases often present as pain during crimping without obvious deformity [DOC-1][DOC-3]. How can I prevent finger injuries when climbing?",
          "citations": {
