    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019ve actually had finger pain like this before, and it really affected my climbing. Are taping techniques effective at preventing these kinds of pulley injuries?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Research shows that taping healthy fingers does not meaningfully reduce the risk of pulley injuries and may even be counterproductive, as it can prevent your pulleys from adapting and strengthening properly (Schweizer, 2000). Taping is most beneficial during recovery from an existing pulley injury, where it can help support the injured area and reduce load, especially when using the H-taping method, which has been shown to be more effective than other techniques in keeping tendons close to the bone and supporting injured pulleys during crimping. H-taping can also increase crimping strength in injured fingers by about 13% and may help prevent further damage while you heal. For prevention, focus on proper warm-up, progressive loading, and good technique rather than taping healthy fingers [DOC-1][DOC-6][DOC-7][DOC-8]. Are you interested in other injury prevention strategies for climbers?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 0.06302805800756621,
