      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yes, I\u2019d like to know about specific warm-up routines or exercises you\u2019d recommend for climbers to avoid finger problems. Anything simple for someone who mainly climbs outdoors?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "To prevent finger injuries when climbing outdoors, start with 10\u201320 minutes of aerobic activity\u2014your approach hike is ideal\u2014to increase blood flow and warm up your muscles and tendons [DOC-1][DOC-3]. Next, perform dynamic stretches for your arms, wrists, and fingers, such as arm circles, wrist rotations, and gentle finger flicks or spreads. Once at the crag, begin with easy climbing: do 8\u201312 easy boulder problems or 3\u20134 easy routes, focusing on smooth movement and technique rather than intensity [DOC-1][DOC-4]. If you have a portable fingerboard or resistance bands, use them for gentle, progressive loading of your fingers before tackling harder problems. Avoid jumping straight onto crimpy or finger-intensive routes, and try to use open-hand or half-crimp grips instead of full crimping when possible to reduce stress on your pulleys [DOC-4]. Are you interested in fingerboard warm-up protocols for outdoor use?",
          "citations": {
