          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "As a climber with a history of finger trauma, distinguishing finger injuries quickly is crucial for your safety and performance. Here\u2019s how to tell the difference between the most common finger injuries relevant to climbing:\n\n**Sprain (Ligament Injury)**\n- **Symptoms:** Pain, swelling, redness, bruising, throbbing. Usually worsens for a few days, then improves with rest.\n- **Mobility:** May have reduced range of motion, but the finger is generally aligned.\n- **Recovery:** Mild sprains resolve in 1\u20132 weeks with RICE (rest, ice, compression, elevation).\n\n**Fracture (Bone Injury)**\n- **Symptoms:** Severe pain, swelling, bruising, possible deformity or abnormal bending. May hear a crack/pop at injury.\n- **Mobility:** Loss of function; finger may be misaligned or impossible to move without extreme pain.\n- **Red Flag:** Symptoms persist or worsen, or you see obvious deformity\u2014seek medical attention and consider an X-ray.\n\n**Tendon Injury (e.g., Mallet Finger)",
