          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Your symptoms\u2014pain in the front of the finger when crimping, no visible bowstringing\u2014strongly suggest a flexor tendon pulley strain, most likely an A2 pulley injury. This is the most common finger injury in outdoor climbers, especially after years of regular climbing and prior trauma.\n\n**Key distinguishing features:**\n- **Pulley (A2) strain:**  \n  - Pain is localized on the palm side (front) of the finger, often at the base.\n  - Triggered or worsened by crimping.\n  - Swelling and tenderness may be present.\n  - Bowstringing (a visible tendon bulge) is absent in mild/moderate cases; its presence signals a severe rupture.\n- **Collateral ligament injury:**  \n  - Pain is on the side of the finger, not the front\u2014less likely in your case.\n- **Flexor tendon rupture:**  \n  - Inability to bend the finger at one or more joints, often after a sudden force or cut\u2014unlikely if you can still move your finger.\n\n**Why this fits",
