          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For an A2 pulley strain\u2014very common in outdoor climbers with your history\u2014the best at-home treatment focuses on protection, gradual rehab, and avoiding reinjury:\n\n**Immediate Care**\n- **Rest:** Stop climbing and avoid crimping or loading the injured finger for at least 1\u20132 weeks.\n- **Ice:** Apply ice for 10\u201315 minutes, 2\u20133 times daily for the first 48 hours to reduce swelling.\n- **Gentle movement:** Begin gentle range-of-motion exercises (bend and straighten the finger to mild tension, not pain) several times a day. This prevents stiffness without stressing the pulley.\n\n**Support**\n- **H-taping:** Use H-taping (proven most effective for pulley support) when you return to climbing. Cut a 10cm x 1.5cm strip of rigid tape, split both ends, and anchor above and below the joint. Retape before each session, as tape stretches quickly.\n- **Splinting:** For moderate pain, consider a finger splint for 1\u20132 weeks.\n\n**Re",
