          "text": "For a climber with your background, distinguishing a serious tendon injury is critical\u2014especially since outdoor climbing and prior trauma increase your risk. Here\u2019s what to watch for:\n\n**Red Flags for Tendon Injuries**\n- **Inability to move the finger normally:**  \n  - *Flexor tendon injury (palm side):* Can\u2019t bend the finger at one or more joints, especially after a cut or sudden force.  \n  - *Extensor tendon injury (back of finger, e.g., mallet finger):* Can\u2019t straighten the fingertip; it droops even if you try.\n- **Sudden \u201cpop\u201d or rip sensation** at the moment of injury, often followed by immediate pain and swelling.\n- **Visible deformity:** Bowstringing (bulge at the base of the finger), finger droop, or abnormal finger position.\n- **Tenderness and swelling** along the tendon path (palm or back of finger).\n- **Numbness** or loss of sensation (may indicate nerve involvement).\n- **Open wounds or deep cuts** over the",
