          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Climbing on an A2 pulley strain without proper rest\u2014especially with your history of finger trauma and regular outdoor climbing\u2014carries significant risks:\n\n**Risks of Climbing Too Soon**\n- **Worsening the Injury:** Continued loading can turn a mild strain into a full rupture, leading to bowstringing, permanent weakness, or chronic pain.\n- **Longer Recovery:** Ignoring rest can double or triple healing time, sometimes requiring months off or even surgery.\n- **Compensation Injuries:** You may unconsciously overload other fingers or the opposite hand, increasing the risk of new injuries.\n- **Loss of Finger Function:** Chronic pulley injuries can reduce grip strength and limit your ability to crimp or hold small edges\u2014critical for outdoor routes.\n\n**Why This Matters for You**\n- Your prior trauma increases susceptibility to reinjury and slower healing.\n- Outdoor climbing often involves unpredictable holds and higher forces, raising the risk of a severe setback.\n\n**Key Takeaway:** Prioritize full, pain-free function before returning. If you feel pressure to climb, consider focusing on technique drills or lower",
