          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For your A2 pulley injury, effective taping is crucial for safe recovery and return to outdoor climbing. Here\u2019s how to maximize support and minimize reinjury risk:\n\n**Best Taping Method: H-Taping**\n- **Why:** Research shows H-taping is the most effective for supporting injured pulleys, keeping tendons close to the bone, and boosting crimping strength in injured fingers (by ~13%)\u2014ideal for climbers with your history (clueweb22-en0033-87-09727, clueweb22-en0007-15-09796).\n- **How to Apply:**\n  - Cut a 10cm x 1.5cm strip of rigid tape (Leukoplast or Leukotape P recommended).\n  - Split both ends lengthwise, leaving a 1cm bridge in the center.\n  - Place the bridge over the injured pulley (usually at the base of the finger, palm side).\n  - Flex your finger to ~60\u00b0, wrap the upper tabs above the joint and the lower tabs below, forming two rings.\n- **",
