      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That helps, thanks. If there\u2019s pain just at the front of the finger when I crimp, but the tendon looks normal and there\u2019s no obvious bowstring, what sort of injury might that be?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Based on your description, it's possible that you have a pulley injury, specifically an A2 pulley injury, which is common in climbers. This type of injury typically causes pain at the base of the finger, especially when crimping, and may not always exhibit obvious signs like bowstringing. However, it's always best to consult a medical professional for a proper diagnosis and treatment. They can assess the injury and provide guidance on the best course of action to take.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:1": 2.513730049133301,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 2.312955617904663,
