      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting\u2014I\u2019ve heard of pulley injuries before! Is it possible to treat this at home, and how long does it usually take to recover enough to climb again?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Pulley injuries can be treated at home, but it's essential to seek a diagnosis from a climbing-knowledgeable physician or orthopedic surgeon first. Treatment typically involves immobilization, gentle range-of-motion exercises, and gradual return to loading and climbing. The recovery time varies depending on the severity of the injury, but with proper treatment, you can expect to be back to light climbing in a few weeks and full-force climbing within a couple of months. It's crucial to avoid pushing through pain and to follow a structured rehabilitation plan to ensure a safe and effective recovery.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0014-37-10189:19": 2.526240348815918,
            "clueweb22-en0006-07-05326:1": 1.779024600982666,
