  {
    "runtag": "orga-llama70b-bm25-minilm-llama70b",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm curious\u2014how do you actually tell the difference between types of finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "The symptoms of a sprained finger can vary greatly depending on the severity of the injury. Common symptoms include pain, swelling, and limited mobility in the affected finger. You may also experience bruising, stiffness",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-96-17361:1": 3.8391878604888916,
            "clueweb22-en0021-76-12845:6": 3.232635498046875,
            "clueweb22-en0034-76-16227:1": 2.916656017303467,
            "clueweb22-en0034-96-17361:0": 1.589742660522461,
            "clueweb22-en0040-99-11570:0": 0.7261452674865723
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s a bit general\u2014could you help me understand how to tell if a finger is sprained, strained, or if it might be a tendon injury? Sometimes when I climb outdoors, it\u2019s hard to know what\u2019s really wrong with my fingers.",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To determine if a finger is sprained, strained, or has a tendon injury, you need to consider the symptoms and how the injury occurred. A sprain typically involves pain, swelling, and limited mobility, often caused by a sudden twisting or bending of the finger. A strain, on the other hand, usually affects the muscle or tendon and is often due to overuse or stretching. Tendon injuries, like mallet finger, typically involve a rupture or injury to the tendon near the fingertip. If you're unsure, it's best to consult a doctor who can assess the injury and provide a proper diagnosis. They may use physical exams, X-rays, or MRI imaging to determine the extent of the injury. Given your climbing activities, it's possible you may have experienced a sprain or strain, but only a medical professional can provide an accurate diagnosis.   Please let me know if I should proceed with the next user query.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0038-43-12079:10": 3.8351316452026367,
            "clueweb22-en0045-52-07239:14": 1.4579806327819824,
            "clueweb22-en0028-41-14099:0": 1.4260890483856201,
            "clueweb22-en0005-08-02273:14": 0.6648522615432739,
            "clueweb22-en0025-95-16003:14": 0.6435351967811584
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s really helpful, thanks. So, if the pain is right in the front of my finger, especially when I\u2019m crimping, but I can\u2019t see any bowstringing, what kind of injury could that be? Sometimes I get this after a tough outdoor climbing session.",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Based on your symptoms, it's possible that you have a pulley strain, which is a common injury among climbers. Pulley strains occur when the tendons and ligaments that support the fingers are stretched or torn, often due to repetitive crimping or gripping. The pain is typically located on the front of the finger, especially when crimping, and can be exacerbated by activities that involve gripping or bending. If you're experiencing persistent pain or discomfort, it's a good idea to consult a doctor or a climbing specialist to get a proper diagnosis and treatment plan. They can help you determine the best course of action to alleviate your symptoms and prevent further injury.   Please go ahead and provide the next user query. I'm ready when you are.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:1": 2.9024081230163574,
            "clueweb22-en0008-49-08589:4": 2.3237063884735107,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": 2.2211296558380127,
            "clueweb22-en0007-75-20030:10": 1.4634215831756592,
            "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:9": 1.4460088014602661
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense\u2014pulley strains do sound familiar in climbing circles. Is there anything specific I can do at home to speed up recovery, or should I completely rest my finger? For instance, I read about taping but I\u2019m not sure how effective it really is.",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To speed up recovery from a pulley strain, it's essential to give your finger rest and avoid activities that aggravate the injury, such as crimping or gripping. Applying ice to the affected area can help reduce pain and inflammation. You can also try using compression bandages or taping to provide support and stability to the finger. However, it's crucial to tape correctly to avoid further injury. Additionally, gentle exercises like finger bends and extensions can help maintain mobility, but should be done carefully to avoid exacerbating the strain. It's also recommended to consult a doctor or a climbing specialist for a proper diagnosis and personalized advice on recovery and rehabilitation. They can provide guidance on the best taping techniques and exercises to aid in your recovery.   Please go ahead and provide the next user query. I'm ready when you are.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0045-52-07239:11": 1.8690273761749268,
            "clueweb22-en0003-11-15042:8": 1.8380212783813477,
            "clueweb22-en0045-52-07239:12": 1.6308294534683228,
            "clueweb22-en0038-23-05701:5": 1.0541527271270752,
            "clueweb22-en0023-05-03412:0": 0.5724986791610718
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s useful. What are the risks if I keep climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the risk if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "If you continue climbing with a pulley strain or other finger injury, you risk prolonging your recovery time and potentially causing further damage. This could lead to increased pain, inflammation, and even long-term disability or deformity. Continued stress on the injured area can also increase the risk of more severe injuries, such as tendon ruptures or chronic conditions like tendinopathy. It's essential to prioritize rest and rehabilitation to allow your finger to heal properly and prevent further complications. Ignoring the injury and continuing to climb can have serious consequences, so it's crucial to take a break and seek medical advice if necessary.   Please go ahead and provide the next user query. I'm ready when you are.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:1": 1.0018494129180908,
            "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 0.9151373505592346,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.1433434635400772,
            "clueweb22-en0012-45-12823:1": -0.39388608932495117,
            "clueweb22-en0006-32-19661:3": -0.41556623578071594
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Understood. When I do start climbing again, what precautions should I take to avoid re-injuring my finger?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "When you start climbing again, it's essential to take precautions to avoid re-injuring your finger. Start with easier routes and gradually increase the difficulty to avoid putting excessive stress on your finger. Warm up thoroughly before climbing, and consider taping your finger for support. Avoid crimping or gripping too hard, and try to use open-hand or half-crimp grips instead. It's also crucial to listen to your body and stop climbing if you experience any pain or discomfort. Additionally, consider incorporating finger exercises and stretches into your training routine to improve flexibility and strength. By taking these precautions, you can reduce the risk of re-injuring your finger and ensure a safe and successful return to climbing.   Please go ahead and provide the next user query. I'm ready when you are.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-30-07587:0": -0.5181290507316589,
            "clueweb22-en0023-98-00232:5": -0.9868261814117432,
            "clueweb22-en0038-33-02567:13": -3.1523444652557373,
            "clueweb22-en0001-34-18428:18": -3.1595022678375244,
            "clueweb22-en0041-39-13152:2": -3.245802879333496
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great tips\u2014thanks. Could you tell me specifics about taping? What\u2019s the best way to tape the front of my finger for this kind of injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To tape the front of your finger for a pulley strain, you can use a method called H-taping. This involves cutting a length of rigid athletic tape and applying it in a specific way to provide support and stability to the finger. The tape should be applied in a way that restricts the movement of the finger at the joint of the injured pulley, helping to prevent further strain. It's essential to retape between each climb, as the tape can stretch and lose its effectiveness. You can also consider consulting a climbing specialist or a doctor for personalized advice on taping and rehabilitation. They can provide guidance on the best taping techniques and exercises to aid in your recovery.   Please let me know if I should proceed with the next user query. I'm ready when you are.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 4.633459091186523,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 3.4832229614257812,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 3.4796814918518066,
