          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That's helpful, thank you. If the pain is mostly at the front of the finger, especially when I'm crimping, but there\u2019s no bowstring visible, does that suggest a particular injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, if the pain is mostly at the front of the finger, especially when you're crimping, but there's no bowstring visible, it could suggest a flexor tendon injury. Flexor tendons are located on the palmar side of the fingers and are responsible for flexing the fingers. When you crimp, you're putting a lot of stress on these tendons, which can lead to inflammation and pain. Without a bowstring, it's less likely to be a flexor tendon rupture, but it's still possible that you have a flexor tendon injury, such as tendinitis or a strain. It's always best to consult a doctor for a proper diagnosis and treatment. They can assess your finger and provide a more accurate diagnosis. They may also recommend imaging tests, such as an MRI, to confirm the diagnosis. In the meantime, you can try to avoid crimping and take regular breaks to rest your fingers. You can also try to modify your climbing",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0038-00-07527:13": -2.365058660507202,
            "clueweb22-en0013-93-15759:18": -2.36720609664917,
