        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That sounds quite likely for me\u2014I've had pain there since last weekend's session. Would icing or warming the finger be better in the evenings?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Icing the finger in the evenings can be beneficial in reducing inflammation and pain. Apply an ice pack to the affected area for 15-20 minutes, several times a day. This can help to reduce swelling and alleviate pain. However, be careful not to apply ice directly to the skin, as this can cause further irritation. Instead, wrap the ice pack in a towel or cloth to protect the skin. Warming the finger may also be helpful, but it's generally recommended to avoid heat in the initial stages of injury, as this can increase blood flow and worsen inflammation. It's best to consult a doctor for personalized advice on managing your specific injury. They can provide guidance on the best course of treatment and help you determine the most effective way to manage your pain and inflammation. In the meantime, you can try to modify your climbing technique to avoid putting further stress on the affected area. This may involve using different grip styles or avoiding crimping altogether. Be sure to listen to your",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:9": 1.113401174545288,
            "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 1.010009527206421,
