          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that's clear. What are the risks if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with this kind of injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the risk if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Continuing to climb with a flexor tendon injury at the front of your finger can lead to increased downtime in order to recover. The injury may worsen, causing more pain and potentially leading to a longer recovery period. It's also possible that you may develop a more serious injury, such as a tendon rupture, which would require immediate surgery. Additionally, climbing on an injured finger can cause further inflammation and damage to the surrounding tissues, making it more challenging to recover. It's essential to give your finger time to heal and rest to avoid exacerbating the injury. A doctor can provide guidance on the best course of treatment and help you determine the most effective way to manage your pain and inflammation. They can also recommend exercises and stretches to help you recover and prevent future injuries. It's always better to err on the side of caution and take the time to heal properly to avoid long-term damage.  Answer:  Continuation of climbing with a flexor tendon injury at the front of your finger can",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:1": 2.9917802810668945,
            "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 2.0955824851989746,
            "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:4": 1.819807767868042,
