          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Understood. What\u2019s some general advice for starting climbing again after resting a finger injury, especially for someone who climbs outdoors?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "When starting climbing again after resting a finger injury, it's essential to begin with gentle and low-intensity activities. Start with shorter climbs and gradually increase the duration and difficulty as your finger becomes more comfortable. It's also crucial to listen to your body and stop climbing if you experience any pain or discomfort. You can also try to modify your climbing technique to avoid putting further stress on the affected area. This may involve using different grip styles or avoiding crimping altogether. Additionally, make sure to warm up properly before climbing, and consider incorporating exercises and stretches to help you recover and prevent future injuries. It's also a good idea to consult a doctor or a physical therapist for personalized advice on managing your specific injury and creating a safe and effective rehabilitation plan. When climbing outdoors, be sure to take regular breaks to rest your fingers and avoid overexertion. It's also essential to be aware of your surroundings and adjust your climbing style accordingly, taking into account factors such as weather, terrain, and",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:12": 0.6295318603515625,
            "clueweb22-en0006-32-19661:3": 0.5921553373336792,
