          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, this is helpful. Back to taping: what\u2019s the best way to tape the front of my finger after this type of injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To tape the front of your finger after a flexor tendon injury, you can start by wrapping the section of the finger closer to the hand first, and then bend the taped joint to 30 degrees and wrap the other section of the finger. This will help to provide support and stability to the injured area. You can also use a figure-eight pattern to secure the tape in place, making sure to apply gentle pressure to avoid further irritating the injury. It's also essential to keep the tape loose enough to allow for a full range of motion, as excessive tension can cause further discomfort and potentially worsen the injury. You can also consider using a specialized finger tape or a pre-made finger wrap to provide additional support and protection. It's always a good idea to consult a doctor or a physical therapist for personalized advice on taping and managing your specific injury. They can provide guidance on the best course of treatment and help you determine the most effective way to manage your pain and inflammation.  Answer:",
          "citations": {
