          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "1. Concerned about finger injuries, likely due to climbing.",
            "2. I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "3. I climb outdoors.",
            "4. Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, thanks! What about if I\u2019ve got pain at the front of my finger, and it really hurts when I crimp, but I don\u2019t see any bowstringing or swelling at the base? Any idea what type of injury that leans toward?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Based on your symptoms\u2014pain at the front of your finger that worsens with crimping, without bowstringing or significant swelling at the base\u2014it strongly suggests a pulley strain. Pulley strains are common in climbers and often cause pain on the front of the finger, especially with crimp grip. The absence of severe symptoms like bowstringing or major swelling indicates it might be a lower-grade injury.",
          "citations": {
