      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That sounds practical. Do you have any recommendations for finger taping techniques after this kind of injury\u2014especially for pain at the front of the finger, near the first joint?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For pulley strains, H-taping is highly effective for pain at the front of your finger. Cut a 10cm x 1.5cm tape, split ends to create a 1cm bridge. Place the bridge on the palmar side of the injured joint crease, flex finger 60\u00b0, and wrap the ends into rings above and below the joint. This method supports the pulley. Remember to retape between climbs as the tape stretches. An alternative is Dr. Julian Saunders' method, which restricts joint movement to prevent crimping.",
