      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m actually looking for some info myself\u2014can you explain ways to tell different finger injuries apart?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "As a climber, differentiating finger injuries involves noting specific symptoms. Pulley injuries, common in climbers, often involve a loud 'pop,' significant swelling and pain, especially at the base of the finger, and sometimes 'bowstringing' (bulging) (Passages 6, 7, 15). Ligament or capsular strains usually cause pain on the side of the joint (Passage 9). Muscle strains, often from fatigue or crimping, result in pain centered on specific hand muscles like interosseus or lumbricals (Passage 9). Fractures present with swelling, tenderness, bruising, and inability to move or deformity (Passage 12). Flexor tendon injuries can cause pain and difficulty bending your finger, or even inability to move it, sometimes with numbness (Passages 12, 20).",
