      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That gives me a good plan. About tape\u2014how should I actually tape my finger after a front-of-finger climbing injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For a front-of-finger climbing injury like a pulley strain, H-taping is recommended for support and preventing reinjury. To H-tape:\n1. Cut a 4-inch piece of athletic tape (or 10cm long, 1.5cm wide).\n2. Tear it lengthwise from both ends, leaving a \u00bd-inch (or 1cm) bridge in the middle.\n3. Place the bridge on the palmar side of the injured finger, at the crease of the joint on the upper end of the injured pulley.\n4. Flex your finger to 30-60 degrees at the joint.\n5. Wrap the upper section of the tape in a ring, then wrap the other section below the joint.\nRemember to retape between each route as tape stretches quickly.",
