        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m actually curious about finger injuries\u2014how can I tell different kinds apart?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To differentiate finger injuries, especially as a climber, look for specific signs. Pulley strains, common in climbers, often cause pain on the front of the finger, a \"pop\" sound, swelling, and sometimes \"bowstringing\" for severe tears. Ligament or capsular strains typically present with pain on the side of the joint. Muscle strains occur when fatigued, with pain centered on specific hand muscles. Beyond climbing-specific injuries, sprains involve ligament damage from hyperextension or jamming, while fractures are direct bone injuries. Mallet finger results in an inability to straighten the fingertip, and dislocations involve specific joint displacement, like a \"jammed finger.\"",
