      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting, thanks! If I feel pain mostly in the front of my finger when I crimp (but there\u2019s no bowstring), what type of injury could that be?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Pain mostly in the front of your finger when crimping, without bowstringing, strongly suggests a pulley strain. Pulley injuries, especially to the A2, are common in climbers and occur from the high forces exerted during crimping. The absence of bowstringing indicates it may not be a severe rupture, but it's still advisable to consult a medical professional for an accurate diagnosis.",
