      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that helps. If I have pain in the front of my finger, especially when crimping, but there's no bowstringing, what kind of injury might that suggest?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Pain in the front of your finger, especially when crimping and without bowstringing, most commonly suggests a pulley strain or injury. Pulley strains, particularly of the A2-A4 pulleys, are frequently associated with a full crimp grip and cause pain on the front of the finger. While severe pulley ruptures can lead to visible bowstringing, less severe strains may not.",
