        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting, I was actually climbing outdoors last weekend\u2014felt the pain start then. Should I stop climbing completely, or is taping and resting between sessions enough for a mild pulley strain?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 4,
          "text": "For a mild pulley strain, it's advised to get a professional diagnosis first to confirm the injury grade. For Grade 1 strains, complete cessation of climbing isn't always necessary. Instead, focus on gentle range-of-motion exercises and avoid pushing through pain. If you continue climbing, it should be lighter, focusing on technique, and strictly pain-free, avoiding holds that aggravate the injury. Taping, particularly H-taping, can offer support during recovery.",
