      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, thanks! If my pain is at the front of the finger, especially when I\u2019m crimping, and I don\u2019t notice any bowstring, what might be going on?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Pain at the front of your finger, especially when crimping, without noticeable bowstringing, most commonly indicates a pulley strain, particularly affecting the A2-A4 pulleys. While crimping increases the risk of these injuries, the absence of bowstringing suggests it may not be a severe rupture.",
