      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. How exactly should I tape my finger for this kind of injury? I hear people use different taping methods\u2014what\u2019s best for pain at the front?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For pulley strains causing pain at the front of your finger, H-taping is highly effective for supporting pulleys, especially when crimping. To apply: Cut a 10cm x 1.5cm rigid athletic tape in half lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1cm middle bridge. Place the bridge on the inside of your finger at the joint crease of the injured pulley. Flex finger 60\u00b0, then wrap upper and lower tape ends around the finger in rings. Dr. Saunders' method is an alternative, immobilizing the finger to prevent crimping.",
