    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Hi, I\u2019m curious about how to tell apart different types of finger injuries. Any tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "great question. quick pointers to differentiate common finger injuries : - fracture / dislocation : crooked finger, obvious deformity, point tenderness on bone, grinding, bruising. numb / cold = emergency. - mallet finger : fingertip droops and can \u2019 t actively straighten ( dip ). needs continuous splinting in extension. - jersey finger : can \u2019 t bend fingertip ( dip ) after grabbing ; often ring finger. surgical evaluation. - boutonniere : middle joint ( pip ) bent, fingertip hyperextended ; pain on top of pip. - volar plate sprain : pain / swelling on palm side of pip after hyperextension ; may develop \u201c swan - neck. \u201d - collateral ligament sprain : pain on sides of a joint, looseness with side - to - side stress ; often treated with buddy taping if stable. - thumb ucl ( \u201c skier \u2019 s \u201d ) : pain at base / inside of thumb, weak pinch ; immobilize in a thumb spica and get assessed. - climber \u2019 s pulley injury : sharp pain / popping at base of finger ( palm side ), tenderness over a2, possible bowstringing. - infections :",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0031-43-02250:0": 0.186728373169899,
            "clueweb22-en0042-29-11551:0": 0.022831454873085022,
            "clueweb22-en0036-39-05218:0": -0.12970393896102905,
            "clueweb22-en0011-74-09063:0": -0.1380769908428192,
            "clueweb22-en0008-72-15164:0": -0.3039911091327667
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful. If I have pain in the front of the finger\u2014especially when crimping\u2014but I don\u2019t see any bowstringing, what kind of injury could that be?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "most likely an a2 pulley strain / partial tear. pulley pain is felt on the palm side at the base of the finger and is provoked by crimping ; absence of bowstringing suggests it \u2019 s not a complete rupture. other possibilities : - volar plate sprain : palm side of the pip after a hyperextension injury ( less likely if crimping is the main trigger ). - collateral ligament sprain : pain more on the sides of the joint, not the front. what to do now : - relative rest from crimping and pockets ; use open - hand grips only. - pulley taping ( h - tape ) can reduce symptoms. - gentle, pain - free range - of - motion and gradual loading ; return only when daily activities and easy climbing are pain - free. - if you felt a pop, have persistent swelling / pain beyond 1 \u2013 2 weeks, weakness, or see any bowstringing, get assessed by a hand / sports - medicine clinician. ultrasound can confirm the diagnosis. prevention when you return : - thorough warm - up : light cardio, dynamic mobility, then easy climbing ( e. g., 100 \u2013 120 easy moves ) before harder efforts. -",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0010-00-05343:6": -4.6886210441589355,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": -4.721718788146973,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": -4.805692672729492,
            "clueweb22-en0011-99-15922:7": -4.814910888671875,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": -4.847280502319336
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That matches what I\u2019m experiencing. The pain is right at the base of my ring finger, and it gets worse after a few hard problems outdoors. No audible pop, just soreness. Is pulley taping easy to do myself?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "yes. h - taping the a2 is easy to do yourself and takes about a minute. - use 0. 5 \u2013 0. 6 inch ( 1. 25 \u2013 1. 5 cm ) athletic / climbing tape. - cut an 8 \u2013 10 cm strip. tear lengthwise from each end to make an \u201c h, \u201d leaving a 1 \u2013 1. 5 cm bridge in the middle. - with the finger slightly flexed ( ~ 30\u00b0 ), place the bridge over the sore spot on the palm side of the proximal phalanx ( just distal to the big knuckle ). - wrap the two proximal tails toward the hand and the two distal tails toward the middle joint ; secure on the back of the finger. keep tape off the joints so they can bend. - it should feel snug and supportive, not tight \u2014 no tingling, numbness, or color change. re - do looser if needed. - optional : add one narrow circumferential \u201c ring \u201d wrap above and below the sore area for extra support. tips : clean / dry skin first, round tape corners, pre - cut a few h - strips for outdoor days. taping helps symptoms but doesn \u2019 t speed healing",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0021-49-08075:1": -1.0641298294067383,
            "clueweb22-en0007-59-12578:2": -2.5727813243865967,
            "clueweb22-en0009-17-06600:3": -2.6657512187957764,
            "clueweb22-en0007-59-12578:3": -2.835341215133667,
            "clueweb22-en0029-60-11175:2": -2.9370667934417725
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s clear, thanks. If I keep climbing and ignore the need for rest, what are the risks for the front-of-finger injury like mine?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the risk if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "short answer : you risk turning a manageable strain into a long, possibly surgical problem. what can happen if you keep pushing : - partial tear \u2192 full a2 ( often a3 too ) rupture with bowstringing, major loss of grip strength ; recovery is months and sometimes needs surgery. - chronic tenosynovitis / synovitis : ongoing swelling, crepitus, \u201c triggering, \u201d morning stiffness. - scar tissue / adhesions : poorer tendon glide, persistent pain, reduced range of motion, higher re - injury risk. - compensatory injuries : other pulleys / volar plate, collateral ligaments, flexor tendons, and even medial elbow tendinopathy. - longer downtime overall : a 2 \u2013 6 week strain can become a months - long setback. if you must keep climbing : - drop intensity / volume ; avoid crimps, pockets, and dynamic moves. use open - hand only. - tape for symptom relief ; stop if pain exceeds mild discomfort or worsens during / after a session. - no hangboarding / campusing until pain - free in daily life and easy climbs. - warm up thoroughly ; climb in warmer conditions to reduce",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0037-56-12062:0": -1.0696173906326294,
            "clueweb22-en0029-60-11175:2": -3.096201181411743,
            "clueweb22-en0022-09-17627:1": -4.380701065063477,
            "clueweb22-en0010-00-05343:6": -4.529167652130127,
            "clueweb22-en0038-58-05511:0": -4.705921649932861
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Makes sense\u2014I definitely don\u2019t want to turn it into something worse. When I\u2019m ready to start climbing again, are there any other precautions I should take besides warming up well and avoiding crimps?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "yes. key precautions for a safe return from an a2 strain : - graded ramp - up : start with very easy mileage, open - hand only. avoid pockets and dynos. favor big feet and static movement. - volume and intensity : cut grade and attempts ~ 50 % for 1 \u2013 2 weeks. cap sessions 45 \u2013 60 minutes. stop at the first twinge. use the 24 - hour rule : if pain > 2 / 10 during / after or worse next morning, back off. - rest spacing : 48 \u2013 72 hours between sessions at first ; no back - to - back days. - tape : use h - tape for symptom relief every session ( doesn \u2019 t speed healing ). - warm conditions : climb on warmer days ; keep hands and forearms warm throughout. - technique focus : precise footwork, hips close, good body positioning to reduce finger load. - rehab loading : daily pain - free tendon glides ; light putty / rice - bucket work. progress to supported open - hand hangs on big edges ( feet on ), short sets, no pain. - avoid until symptom - free : hangboarding on small edges, campusing, hard boulders, and any crimping.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0041-76-16536:1": -1.8188250064849854,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": -4.422708511352539,
            "clueweb22-en0041-76-16536:2": -4.452519416809082,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": -4.472196578979492,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": -4.522699356079102
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great advice. Are there any long-term changes I should consider in my training to help prevent finger injuries from happening again?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "yes \u2014 think long - term load management, balanced strength, and smarter skill work. - periodize : 1 \u2013 2 hard sessions / week max, 48 \u2013 72 h between finger - intense days. deload every 4th \u2013 6th week. - progressive finger training : 1 \u2013 2 hangboard sessions / week using open - hand / half - crimp on larger edges. small, steady progress ( < 5 \u2013 10 % per week ). save full crimp for sends, not training. - grip variety : build slopers, pinches, and half - crimp so you \u2019 re not dependent on tiny crimps. - antagonists / balance : 2 \u2013 3x / week extensor band work, wrist extensor eccentrics, pronation / supination, finger intrinsics ( rice bucket / putty ), plus scapular and rotator - cuff strength. - mobility / tendon health : daily tendon glides, gentle wrist / finger mobility, forearm soft - tissue care. - technique and pacing : prioritize precise footwork, hips - in, static movement ; fewer max - effort attempts, longer rests between tries. limit dynos / pockets. - warmth : climb",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-16-19141:3": -0.946239709854126,
