      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks for the detailed suggestions. I tend to climb outside more when it\u2019s warm anyway\u2014helps the fingers. By the way, do you know if previous injuries, like an old bouldering fall, make finger injuries more likely?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "short answer : yes, but it depends. - highest risk : a prior finger / pulley injury \u2014 reinjury rates are real. - old non - finger injuries ( e. g., from a bouldering fall ) raise finger risk mainly if they left you with shoulder / elbow / wrist stiffness, weakness, or altered technique. those deficits shift load to the fingers, especially on the ring finger in half / full crimp. - age and desk time add to risk via stiffer tendons and slower adaptation. what to do : - screen asymmetries : compare shoulder external rotation, scapular control, wrist extension ( aim ~ 70 \u2013 80\u00b0 ), grip strength side to side. address deficits before ramping intensity. - strengthen the \u201c upstream \u201d : 2 \u2013 3x / week scapular stabilizers and rotator cuff ; wrist extensor eccentrics ; forearm pronation / supination ; finger extensors. - mobility / health : daily tendon glides, gentle wrist / finger mobility, forearm soft - tissue work. take movement breaks at your desk ; a sit - stand setup helps. - load management : avoid big weekend spikes when it \u2019 s warm ; keep weekly",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0035-68-15480:19": -2.1011250019073486,
