      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting, I hadn\u2019t really linked the old shoulder issues from my fall with my finger problems, but it makes sense. If my left (dominant) hand is more affected, should I modify anything during recovery?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "yes \u2014 because it \u2019 s your dominant, left hand, be extra intentional about reducing day - to - day load while you heal. what to change now - use your right hand for heavy gripping : carrying bags, opening jars / doors, lifting kids, bike maintenance. - at your desk : switch the mouse to the right, use a light - click / vertical mouse, keep keystrokes soft, consider a split / low - force keyboard, and try voice dictation for longer typing. take 1 \u2013 2 minute movement breaks hourly with tendon glides. - phone : swap to right - hand use when possible ; avoid prolonged tight grips. - warmth : keep the hand warm ; brief warm - water soaks before activity help. climbing / training tweaks - choose problems that don \u2019 t require hard left - hand crimping, pockets, or dynamic moves. open - hand only. - tape the a2 for sessions ; stop if pain > mild. - avoid left one - arm hangs, small - edge hangs, campusing. use feet - on, big - edge isometrics only if completely pain - free. - don \u2019 t overcompensate with the right \u2014 cap total volume and alternate sides to prevent",
