      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, thanks. If I get pain in the front of the finger when crimping, but can\u2019t see any bowstring\u2014what could be wrong? It\u2019s mostly when I\u2019m climbing crimpy routes.",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "sounds like a climbing pulley / flexor - tendon problem rather than a joint or fracture. common causes when crimping without obvious bowstringing : - partial pulley strain / tear ( especially a2 ) : pain at the base of the finger with crimping, focal tenderness, worse with resisted bending. no bowstringing with partial tears. - tendon sheath irritation / flexor tendinopathy or tenosynovitis : pain with load, less focal swelling. - less likely : early / small pulley rupture, small avulsion fracture, or referred pain from nearby structures. what to do now - stop crimping and avoid heavy loading on that finger. - ice, nsaids for symptom control, and rest. - consider pulley taping or a ring - type support to offload while climbing and use open - hand grip. - if you hear a loud pop, can \u2019 t bend / straighten the finger, develop obvious deformity, numbness, or pain that doesn \u2019 t improve in a week \u2013 10 days, get urgent evaluation ( hand / orthopedic or sports clinician ) and imaging ( us / mri / x - ray ) to rule out complete rupt",
          "citations": {
