          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s really clear, thanks! What\u2019s the risk if I just keep climbing like normal and don\u2019t rest the finger\u2014even if it hurts at the front?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the risk if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "If you keep climbing through front-of-finger pain, you risk turning a mild, recoverable problem into a much worse one. Specific risks: - A partial A2 pulley strain can progress to a larger rupture (and eventually bowstringing) if repeatedly loaded, which may require surgery and a much longer rehab. - Worsening tendinopathy or tenosynovitis with chronic pain, stiffness, loss of strength and prolonged downtime (often months). - Increased chance of associated injuries (bigger pulley tears, small avulsion fractures) from repeated high forces\u2014full crimping greatly increases those forces. - Slower, more complicated recovery with scar tissue, weakness, or recurrent problems.  What to do now: stop crimping and avoid heavy loading on that finger, use open-hand/half-crimp, consider pulley taping or a ring support, ice/NSAIDs for symptoms, do only pain-free mobility, and avoid hangboard/weighted hangs. See a hand/ sports clinician and get imaging if you hear a pop, lose motion, get numbness, or there\u2019s no improvement in ~7\u201310 days.",
