          "text": "good \u2014 sensible to be cautious. practical return plan : - get pain \u2011 free range of motion first ( often 1 \u2013 2 weeks ). if you can \u2019 t regain motion or pain is severe, see a hand / sports clinician. - while still sore : avoid crimping / hard loads. use open \u2011 hand or half \u2011 crimp, pulley taping or a ring support, and avoid hangboard / weighted hangs. - do gentle, pain \u2011 free mobility and forearm self \u2011 massage to reduce stiffness and scar formation. ice / nsaids as needed. - when you have several days with minimal / zero pain, begin progressive, controlled strengthening : finger extensor work ( bands ), light grippers, and very low \u2011 intensity hangboard work with good warm \u2011 up and lots of foot assistance. start with high volume / low intensity on easier routes ( 2 \u2013 3 climbs well below your max ) and slowly add intensity. - incorporate extensor exercises regularly to rebalance forearm musculature and reduce reinjury risk. - red flags : an audible pop, loss of motion, numbness, or no improvement in ~ 7 \u2013 10 days \u2014 get urgent evaluation and imaging. progress slowly, listen to",
