          "text": "short answer : use an h \u2011 style pulley tape ( research shows it gives the best off \u2011 loading ) and be careful not to make it too tight. if you \u2019 re unsure, get a clinician / coach to show you in person. how to do a basic h \u2011 tape ( conceptual steps ) - materials : non \u2011 elastic climbing tape, scissors. clean / dry skin. - anchor 1 : one snug ( not tight ) ring of tape around the proximal phalanx just below the finger base. - anchor 2 : a second ring around the middle phalanx just above the pip joint. - bridge / \u201c h \u201d bar : place a short strip on the palm ( volar ) side connecting the two anchors across the injured pulley area so it presses the tendon toward the bone. add small side strips to secure if needed. - check : capillary refill and sensation after taping ; it must not feel numb, cold, or unusually painful. practical tips - use tape only while climbing / recovering, not as permanent prevention. - remove immediately if numbness, color change, increasing pain. - stop crimping, favor open \u2011 hand, ice / nsaids, and see a hand",
