          "user_utterance": "I'm looking for ways to tell different finger injuries apart. What are the main types?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "here are the common finger - injury types and how to tell them apart : - mallet finger ( extensor tendon injury ) : fingertip droops and you cannot actively straighten the distal joint ( dip ). often follows a ball impact ; may have bruising, tenderness, nail - bed injury or a small avulsion fracture. - dislocation : obvious joint deformity, marked pain, swelling and inability to move the joint. may cause numbness / tingling if a nearby nerve is injured. x \u2011 ray needed to check for associated fracture. - sprain ( ligament stretch / tear ) : pain, swelling, and joint stiffness or looseness after a twist or hit but usually less deformity than a dislocation. often managed with splinting or buddy taping. - fracture : sharp pain, swelling, deformity, focal bony tenderness, and reduced motion. x \u2011 ray confirms fracture type ( including crush or intraarticular ). - nerve / neuropathy ( e. g., carpal tunnel, systemic causes ) : numbness, tingling, burning or shooting pain in fingers, often without acute trauma and sometimes worse at night. seek urgent care for severe deformity, obvious",
