  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini-nopersonal",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Okay, got it. I haven\u2019t noticed any popping sound, but it gets sore after some climbs\u2014especially crimpy ones. Would gentle stretching or finger exercises help, or should I avoid that for now?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Gentle, pain\u2011free mobility work is fine and often helpful, but avoid loading or stretching into pain. Practical plan:  - Immediately: stop crimping and avoid heavy loading on that finger. Use open\u2011hand or half\u2011crimp, consider pulley taping or a ring support while climbing. Ice, rest, and NSAIDs for symptoms.   - Short term (while sore): do gentle active range\u2011of\u2011motion (bend/straighten fingers), light pain\u2011free finger stretches, and forearm massage to increase blood flow. Avoid hangboard, weighted hangs, or hard crimping until pain settles.   - When pain is minimal/absent for several days: start progressive, controlled strengthening \u2014 finger extensor work with resistance bands, light grippers, and very low-intensity hangboard protocol (well warmed up and gradual).   - See a clinician urgently if you hear a pop, lose motion, get numbness, or if pain doesn\u2019t improve within ~7\u201310 days \u2014 they may need imaging to rule out bigger pulley or tendon injury.  Listen to your body and progress slowly.",
          "citations": {
