  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "3-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m looking for ways to tell different finger injuries apart. Any tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "good question \u2014 different finger injuries often produce distinct patterns. quick ways to tell them apart : - mallet finger : pain at fingertip, droop of the distal joint ( dip ) and trouble extending the tip. - boutonniere deformity : pip joint looks bent ( flexed ) while dip may hyperextend \u2014 often from a central slip injury. - dislocation : obvious crooked / deformed finger, severe pain, and marked loss of motion. - fracture : focal bony tenderness, swelling, bruising, possible deformity ; pain with pressure or movement. x - ray often needed. - sprain / ligament strain : swollen, sore joint but usually less deformity than a fracture ; painful with certain movements. - trigger finger : catching or locking when you bend / straighten, often a painless nodule at the base. - nail - bed / subungual hematoma : dark blood under the nail after crush injury. - infection ( paronychia / tenosynovitis ) : redness, warmth, increasing pain, pus, sometimes fever. immediate steps : immobilise ( splint or buddy - tape ), ice, elevate, and avoid forcing motion",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-22-15894:0": -0.20863106846809387,
            "clueweb22-en0016-45-08635:0": -0.2119605541229248,
            "clueweb22-en0030-12-13652:1": -0.3598383963108063,
            "clueweb22-en0018-52-12259:0": -0.44208449125289917,
            "clueweb22-en0004-65-03089:0": -0.4681479334831238
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that helps. If the pain is at the front of my finger when I\u2019m crimping, but I\u2019m not seeing any bowstringing, what kind of injury could that be?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but I cannot see any bowstring. What injury could this be?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "That pattern \u2014 pain on the front (volar) surface when crimping but no bowstringing \u2014 is most consistent with a pulley strain/partial tear (A2 is the common one in climbers). A full A2\u2013A4 rupture usually produces visible bowstringing, so absence of that makes a complete rupture less likely.  What to do now - Offload: avoid full crimping and heavy finger loading; use open-hand or larger holds.   - Short-term care: rest, ice, elevate, and consider NSAIDs for pain.   - Protection: pulley-taping or a supportive splint can reduce load while healing.   - Rehab: gradual finger-strengthening and range-of-motion work once pain subsides.   - See a clinician if you have severe swelling, inability to bend the finger, increasing pain, or if symptoms don\u2019t improve in 1\u20132 weeks \u2014 they may image or refer for specialist care.  Also review warm-up and technique to reduce recurrence.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-47-10871:0": -0.9609547257423401,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": -0.9966484308242798,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": -1.5303149223327637,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": -2.596346378326416,
            "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": -2.85121488571167
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That sounds familiar with what I've felt. How long does it usually take to heal if it's a pulley strain?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I cure the pain in the front of my finger considering I hurt myself while climbing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "If it\u2019s a simple pulley strain (grade 1) you can expect recovery in about 1\u20133 weeks with rest, gentle range-of-motion, and avoiding full crimping. A partial tear (grade 2) takes longer: immobilize 1\u20132 weeks, then gradual ROM and strengthening, and use H\u2011taping while climbing for roughly 3 months \u2014 full return usually occurs over several weeks to a few months. More severe complete tears (grade 3) also start with 1\u20132 weeks immobilization but typically need longer protection/h\u2011taping (about 6 months) and sometimes surgery for grade 4 injuries.  Practical steps: offload the finger (no full crimps), ice, short-term immobilization if needed, then progressive strengthening only when pain-free. See a clinician sooner if you get severe swelling, loss of motion, visible bowstringing, or no improvement in 1\u20132 weeks.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": -2.3995299339294434,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": -3.0003857612609863,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": -3.4839186668395996,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": -3.6679787635803223,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": -4.125321388244629
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "If I keep climbing and don\u2019t rest, what kinds of risks am I looking at with this injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the risk if I continue climbing and don\u2019t rest with my injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "If you keep climbing without rest you risk making a minor pulley strain far worse. Possible consequences: - Progression from strain \u2192 partial tear \u2192 complete rupture, with much longer recovery and sometimes surgery.   - Bowstringing (tendon pulls away from bone), visible bulging, loss of finger function and weaker grip.   - Prolonged pain, swelling and stiffness that can take months to recover and predispose to repeated injury.   - Reduced climbing performance and possible chronic instability or deformity of the finger.  If you must climb, avoid full crimps, reduce load, warm up well, and use protective taping (H\u2011taping is most supportive) or a splint \u2014 but tape is a temporary measure and not a substitute for rest. Stop and see a clinician if you get increased pain, growing swelling, inability to bend the finger, or any visible bowstringing.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0005-75-08221:3": -4.261097431182861,
            "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:6": -4.603626251220703,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": -4.6668782234191895,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": -5.559202671051025,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": -5.742040634155273
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm thinking about when to start again. Any specific advice for restarting climbing after a finger injury, especially if I'm keen to get back outdoors?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is some advice for when I start again climbing considering that I had a finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "good plan to be cautious. practical restart steps : - minimum criteria before returning : full, pain - free range of motion ; no swelling ; painless resisted finger flexion ; and no pain on normal holds or light hangboard work using your feet. - timelines ( rough guide ) : grade 1 \u2192 often 1 \u2013 3 weeks ; grade 2 \u2192 several weeks to months of protection / rehab ; grade 3 + \u2192 months and possible specialist care. - progression : begin with easy routes, lots of feet, and open - hand grips only. gradually add load : open - hand \u2192 half - crimp \u2192 short, controlled crimps only when entirely pain - free. limit volume and avoid hard moves for several weeks. - strength work : start gentle rom and then progressive loading ( hangboard / devices ) only once pain - free ; always use feet and avoid crimping early. - protection and pain control : consider h \u2011 taping when climbing ( most supportive ), temporary splinting if needed, ice, and nsaids as appropriate. - ongoing care : massage forearm flexors / myofascial work to prevent adhesions. stop and see a clinician if pain, swelling, loss of motion, or bowstringing",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": -0.8020131587982178,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": -1.071134090423584,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": -1.0783157348632812,
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": -1.1009012460708618,
            "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": -1.3522412776947021
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s really clear\u2014thanks. Any particular taping technique you recommend for the front of the finger, especially after pulley injuries?",
          "user_rubrik": "How should I tape my fingers after my climbing injury at the front of my finger?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "H\u2011taping is the one most supported by research for pulley injuries \u2014 it helps hold the tendons close to the bone and reduces load while you recover. Practical points:  - Purpose: extra support, partial immobilisation (reduce crimping), and a reminder to protect the finger. Use it while climbing after an injury, not as a preventive for healthy fingers.   - When to use: grade\u20112 injuries typically taped while climbing for ~3 months; grade\u20111 you may only need short protection; see a clinician for severe problems or no improvement.   - How to do a basic H\u2011tape (get hands\u2011on instruction from a physio or trusted video if unsure): clean/dry skin; place an anchor loop proximal and one distal to the injured pulley; with the finger slightly flexed, apply two vertical strips along either side of the finger crossing the volar surface to form an \u201cH\u201d that presses the tendon toward bone. Don\u2019t make it too tight \u2014 check capillary refill and sensation.   - Watch for increased pain, numbness, or swelling; if that happens or symptoms persist, stop and see a clinician.",
