      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m looking for ways to tell different finger injuries apart. Any tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I distinguish different finger injuries?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "good question \u2014 different finger injuries often produce distinct patterns. quick ways to tell them apart : - mallet finger : pain at fingertip, droop of the distal joint ( dip ) and trouble extending the tip. - boutonniere deformity : pip joint looks bent ( flexed ) while dip may hyperextend \u2014 often from a central slip injury. - dislocation : obvious crooked / deformed finger, severe pain, and marked loss of motion. - fracture : focal bony tenderness, swelling, bruising, possible deformity ; pain with pressure or movement. x - ray often needed. - sprain / ligament strain : swollen, sore joint but usually less deformity than a fracture ; painful with certain movements. - trigger finger : catching or locking when you bend / straighten, often a painless nodule at the base. - nail - bed / subungual hematoma : dark blood under the nail after crush injury. - infection ( paronychia / tenosynovitis ) : redness, warmth, increasing pain, pus, sometimes fever. immediate steps : immobilise ( splint or buddy - tape ), ice, elevate, and avoid forcing motion",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-22-15894:0": -0.20863106846809387,
